==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|mouch}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|moochen}}, {{m|enm|mouchen|t=to pretend poverty}}, from {{der|en|fro|muchier}}, {{m|fro|mucier}}, {{m|fro|mucer|t=to skulk, hide, conceal}}, from {{der|en|frk|*mukjan|t=to hide, conceal oneself}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*mūk-|*mukjaną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*mūk-|*mūkōną|t=to hide, ambush}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)mūg-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*(s)mewgʰ-|t=swindler, thief}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|mūhhōn|t=to store, cache, plunder}}, {{cog|gmh|muchen}}, {{m|gmh|mucken|t=to hide, stash}}, {{cog|enm|muchen|müchen}}, {{m|enm|michen|t=to rob, steal, pilfer}}. More at {{l|en|mitch}}.

Alternate etymology derives ''mooch'' from {{inh|en|enm|mucchen|t=to hoard, be stingy|lit=to hide coins in one's nightcap}}, from {{der|en|enm|mucche|t=nightcap}}, from {{der|en|dum|mutse|t=cap, nightcap}}, from {{der|en|ML.|almucia|t=nightcap}}, of unknown origin, possibly Arabic. More at {{l|en|mutch}}, {{l|en|amice}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/muːtʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-mooch.ogg|Audio (US)}}
*: {{rhymes|en|uːtʃ}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|es}}

# {{lb|en|British}} To [[wander]] around [[aimlessly]], often causing irritation to others.
#* '''2019''', Barney Ronay, ''Liverpool’s waves of red fury and recklessness end in joyous bedlam'' (in ''The Guardian'', 8 May 2019)[https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/07/liverpool-fury-recklessness-bedlam-champions-league-barcelona]
#*: With 79 minutes gone, the most celebrated team of the modern age had been reduced to bunch of '''mooching''', stumbling yellow-shirted spectators.
#*: These chaps that '''mooch''' about, as Hyde was doing, pick up all sorts of odds and ends. He may have pinched them from a chemist’s shop.
# To [[beg]], [[cadge]], or [[sponge]]; to [[exploit]] or [[take advantage]] of others for personal gain.
#* '''1990''', p. 26, Michael L. Frankel & friends, ''Gently with the Tides'', Center for Marine Conservation, Washington (DC), {{ISBN|1879269-007}}, p. 26,
#*: I managed to '''mooch''' my way up the journalistic ladder to the next, more impressive level of “Interviewer”.
# {{lb|en|British}} To [[steal]] or [[filch]].

